name: type-ascription-option
description: "case _: Option[?] holds for Some and for None on every lane"
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# `case _: Option[?]`

`BUGS.md` `type-ascription-option-missing-arm`. Found by the type-ascription census
(`specs/type-ascription-matrix.md`): `case _: Option[?]` answered nothing on the native and js lanes
where INT and the JVM answered `Option`.

A missing ARM, not a missing type — which is why it could be closed. Both lanes represent an Option
distinctly: `DataV("Some"/"None")` on the native lane, and `_type` markers on js
(`const None = {_type: '_None'}`). Contrast `Set`, `List` and `Vector`, which are all the same plain
array on js and all the same list on native — those cannot be separated by any arm, and the census
records them as representation gaps instead.

```scalascript
def kind(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: Option[?] => "Option"
  case _ => "-"

println(kind(Some(1)))
println(kind(None))
println(kind(Some(Some(2))))
println(kind(List(1, 2)))
println(kind("s"))
println(kind(7))

def unwrap(o: Option[Int]): Int = o match
  case Some(v) => v
  case None => -1
println(unwrap(Some(9)))
println(unwrap(None))
```

**`None` matters on its own line.** It is a different tag from `Some` reaching the same test, and on
js it is a shared singleton object — a truthiness-based test would pass `Some` and fail `None`, or
the reverse, and a case carrying only `Some` could not tell.

The two `unwrap` lines are the guard that widening the TYPE test left the CONSTRUCTOR patterns
alone: `case Some(v)` still arrives naming its own tag and must keep taking the ordinary path.
